doing it and it can be muddling because it's almost like there's too many different ways of doing

00:14.880 --> 00:19.600
something that's quite similar, which is just sort of taking checkpoints of how you're going or

00:19.600 --> 00:23.839
being able to get back to where you were before. There's a few different ways of doing it and I'm

00:23.839 --> 00:30.320
going to explain them and how they fit together. And then I'm going to tell you how I do it myself. You could do it the way I do it or do it your own way.

00:30.320 --> 00:32.320
That's the whole idea of it being flexible.

00:32.320 --> 00:39.600
Okay, so Claude has two levels of granularity of how you can think about the journey you're having with Claude

00:39.600 --> 00:41.759
and go back to different points in time.

00:41.759 --> 00:49.320
And the first of them, the highest level of granularity are things called sessions. And a session represents a complete state

00:49.320 --> 00:51.159
of your conversation with Claude,

00:51.159 --> 00:54.159
of the context with the conversation's got in it,

00:54.159 --> 00:55.679
and other stuff you're doing.

00:55.679 --> 00:57.240
That is the session.

00:57.240 --> 00:59.920
And you can name your session at any point,

00:59.920 --> 01:02.460
give it a name, and then that sort of records

01:02.460 --> 01:05.079
the state of the session at that point when you named it.

01:05.079 --> 01:07.560
And then later you can choose to resume

01:07.560 --> 01:09.799
from any named session,

01:09.799 --> 01:12.480
and it will go back to that state of the context

01:12.480 --> 01:17.840
at that point. And that is a session. In fact, you can just choose to resume Claude from wherever

01:17.840 --> 01:22.239
you were when you lost quick chord. Claude, this is so that's just resume from where I just was,

01:22.799 --> 01:27.760
or you can name a session and resume from that session. That's the idea behind a session.

01:27.760 --> 01:32.000
That sounds like the easy one. That makes total sense, right? Just worth noting that when you do that,

01:32.000 --> 01:34.040
you're resuming to the conversation.

01:34.040 --> 01:36.560
You're not resuming to the state of the code

01:36.560 --> 01:38.060
and the repo at that point,

01:38.060 --> 01:39.840
just the context and conversation.

01:39.840 --> 01:41.040
All right, hopefully that's clear.

01:41.040 --> 01:42.760
So you're thinking, what's the other one then?

01:42.760 --> 01:44.079
That can't be anything more than that.

01:44.079 --> 01:44.879
There is something else.

01:44.879 --> 01:46.799
There's something called checkpointing,

01:46.799 --> 01:49.719
and checkpointing applies to a specific session,

01:49.719 --> 01:51.120
the session that you're currently on.

01:51.120 --> 01:53.040
You're there using Cloud Code.

01:53.040 --> 01:55.120
Every single prompt that you send to

01:55.120 --> 02:01.920
Cloud Code, every one of those is considered a step in time. Each prompt, each one is considered a

02:01.920 --> 02:09.199
checkpoint. And you can do what's called rewinding, which is going back step by step in the conversation

02:09.199 --> 02:14.240
you're having to that point in time. And that process of having this step by step prompt and

02:14.240 --> 02:16.840
being able to rewind is called check pointing.

02:16.840 --> 02:21.699
And you can see it's a much finer granularity than sessions, which are big things.

02:21.699 --> 02:26.039
But the nice thing about these check points you can rewind is that you can choose, do I

02:26.039 --> 02:30.240
just want to rewind just the conversation to go back to where I was with Claude, or do

02:30.240 --> 02:35.000
I want to actually revert the code back to where it was back then?

02:35.000 --> 02:39.379
If Claude made a bunch of changes, I wanted to undo those changes and get back

02:39.379 --> 02:43.740
to the state, how is that right there? Then you can do that. And that's pretty cool.

02:43.740 --> 02:49.340
But there is a catch. When you go back, Claude knows about the changes it's just made, and

02:49.340 --> 02:53.180
those are the ones that it can revert to take you back to where you were.

02:53.180 --> 02:59.439
But if as part of this particular prompt, Claude ran a script, say, on your computer,

02:59.439 --> 03:00.719
and that script did a few things,

03:00.719 --> 03:02.639
which included changing some files.

03:02.639 --> 03:04.400
Then Claude can't revert those.

03:04.400 --> 03:05.599
It doesn't know about those.

03:05.599 --> 03:07.159
Or if you did something that ran a script

03:07.159 --> 03:08.840
that made some changes to files,

03:08.840 --> 03:11.319
Claude isn't actually keeping like a copy of your disk

03:11.319 --> 03:12.599
at each of these points.

03:12.599 --> 03:15.360
So all it can do is undo the changes it knows about.

03:15.360 --> 03:18.080
So you have to be quite, quite aware of what's going on

03:18.080 --> 03:20.080
and exactly how you're going backwards

03:20.080 --> 03:22.479
if you choose to go backwards in terms of the code,

03:22.479 --> 03:26.879
as well as the conversation. But that's the difference between the session and the check

03:26.879 --> 03:31.759
pointing, session resuming, checkpoint rewinding. It can be a bit confusing until you've heard it

03:31.759 --> 03:35.360
clearly like this and you've thought about it and you've got it clear in your mind, just always

03:35.360 --> 03:39.120
always come back to this if you're doing one or the other to be clear on what you're doing and we

03:39.120 --> 03:43.120
will try it out both. But there is something else as well. There is something else. And hopefully

